{
  "description": "Golden table for keyboard occlusion on interaction tap points (#2589), consumed by packages/contracts/src/tap-keyboard-occlusion.ts. A visible software keyboard is its own system surface: it is never a covering sibling of app content, so the same-window occlusion classifier cannot see it, and it leaves the app window rect intact, so the viewport rule passes a covered tab bar. The band runs from the topmost keyboard node down to the bottom of the viewport, across the keyboard's own columns; the tap point is the target's rect center, matching isTapPointInsideViewport. `nodes` are captured snapshot nodes as they appear in the projection the interaction resolved against — note that regular and interactive projections prune the keyboard's own container, leaving Key nodes beside app chrome. The band is derived only from keyboard geometry that is docked to the viewport bottom edge: measured on iPhone 17 Pro (iOS 26.2) the reported key plane stops 58 pt short of an 874 pt portrait viewport and lands exactly on a 402 pt landscape one, so an 80 pt docking budget is what separates a docked keyboard from an iPad floating or split keyboard, whose band stays unmeasured and fails open. `target` names either a resolved node index or a bare coordinate point; both paths read one decision and choose their own consequence.",
